<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<xri version="1.0">
   <description>
      <p>astro-pi — unified PixInsight distribution (scarter4work)</p>
      <p>NukeX stacking and stretching module, PI Copilot assistant panel, Planetary video viewer and stacker, Gaia Depth Grade script and its sidecar program, and the EZ Stretch BSC scripts.</p>
      <p>Repository: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/scarter4work/astro-pi/main/repository/</p>
   </description>
   <platform os="linux" arch="x64" version="1.8.0:2.0.0">
      <package fileName="20261013-linux-x64-NukeX-5.2.0.1.tar.gz" sha1="07f040784c93acf931c76439f640e59b12f22830" type="module" releaseDate="20261028">
         <title>NukeX</title>
         <description>
            <p>
               <b>NukeX 5.2.0.1</b>
            </p>
            <p>Distribution-fitted stacking and auto-stretch for PixInsight. NukeX stacks the FITS light frames you add (with optional flats): it fits a statistical distribution to every pixel&apos;s samples across the stack, estimates the signal from the fitted model, and applies one of its stretches (Auto is recommended). A camera quantum-efficiency (QE) database built into the module routes each frame and decomposes dual-narrowband colour data (HaO3 / S2O3) into emission lines. It loads on any Linux with glibc 2.34 or newer (Ubuntu 22.04+, Debian 12+, Rocky/RHEL 9+).</p>
            <p>Network: one kind of connection only, the camera-database update check. It makes HTTPS requests to raw.githubusercontent.com (this repository), automatically at most once every 7 days when you open the NukeX window, and whenever you press Check now; it sends no identifier, usage data or image data. A newer database is downloaded and installed only if you agree, after its Ed25519 signatures are verified. Automatic checks can be turned off in the NukeX window. NukeX launches no external program. Files outside the PixInsight installation: ~/.config/nukex4/ (update-check state, a downloaded database, filter names you teach it, your stretch ratings), a frame cache in ~/.cache/nukex4/cache/ while a run lasts, and progress files in /tmp. Documentation, including every network connection, external program and file location: doc/tools/NukeX/NukeX.html.</p>
         </description>
      </package>
      <package fileName="20261024-linux-x64-PICopilot-0.2.2.1.tar.gz" sha1="a527699a9aa009f6e9d53adf777c3f9f4c874624" type="module" releaseDate="20261028">
         <title>PI Copilot</title>
         <description>
            <p>
               <b>PI Copilot 0.2.2.1</b>
            </p>
            <p>A chat panel for PixInsight: you talk to Claude, an AI model made by Anthropic, with your own Anthropic API key. It can see the active image (a small preview, its statistics and keywords) and, depending on the mode (Copilot, Guided, or the read-only Advisor), run PixInsight processes on your images, start installed and signed PixInsight scripts that you confirm, and record how each image was processed. It never runs a script written by the model; it can only start installed, signed scripts you confirm.</p>
            <p>Network: HTTPS to api.anthropic.com only, and nothing is sent until you agree in its consent dialog. External programs: secret-tool, run through /bin/sh and /usr/bin/env, keeps the API key in the system keyring; PixInsight&apos;s GraXpert process, when the model runs it, starts the GraXpert program you chose in GraXpert&apos;s own settings. Files outside the PixInsight installation: the journey library in ~/.local/share/PICopilot/journeys/, short-lived files in the system temporary directory, and an export folder only if you choose one. Documentation, including every network connection, external program and file location: doc/tools/PICopilot/PICopilot.html.</p>
         </description>
      </package>
      <package fileName="20261013-linux-x64-GaiaDepthGradeSidecar-1.0.8.tar.gz" sha1="9bb92ab3b6c336ccd7158ab3c2eb77df8ca697cf" type="generic" releaseDate="20261028">
         <title>Gaia Depth Grade sidecar</title>
         <description>
            <p>
               <b>Gaia Depth Grade sidecar 1.0.8</b>
            </p>
            <p>The image-processing program used by the Gaia Depth Grade script (install both). It is installed as bin/gaia-depth-grade-sidecar and its licences in doc/scripts/GaiaDepthGrade/. It is a self-contained program (Python with NumPy, SciPy, Astropy, photutils, astroquery and Pillow); no Python installation is needed.</p>
            <p>Delivered by PixInsight&apos;s update system (earlier versions were downloaded by the script). It runs on any Linux that runs PixInsight with glibc 2.34 or newer (Ubuntu 22.04+, Debian 12+, Rocky/RHEL 9+).</p>
         </description>
      </package>
      <package fileName="20261028-linux-x64-Planetary-0.4.0.0.tar.gz" sha1="f1460b497ac1d3a59133e30edee3374b7139afd8" type="module" releaseDate="20261028">
         <title>Planetary</title>
         <description>
            <p>
               <b>Planetary 0.4.0.0</b>
            </p>
            <p>Planetary imaging for PixInsight, with two processes. SERViewer opens SER video files and uncompressed 8-bit greyscale or raw Bayer AVI files (such as ASIAIR captures): it shows any frame in a preview, opens it as a PixInsight image, and can save an AVI as a SER file. PlanetaryStack stacks such a video into one image (lucky imaging): it measures every frame, chooses the sharpest frames and aligns them, region by region, with nothing to set, previews the stack with zoom, and opens it as a new image, or says why a capture gives none. New in 0.4: the target is read from the ASIAIR file name, and for a broadband colour capture of the Moon PlanetaryStack can also make a mineral moon (calibrated, sharpened, with the Moon&apos;s true colour differences amplified), tuned live with Strength, Chroma blur and Sharpen sliders.</p>
            <p>Both read only the video file you choose and an AVI&apos;s .txt sidecar. SERViewer writes only the SER file you choose; either process writes, if you click Save pattern..., the .txt sidecar next to the AVI. Neither uses the network or runs external programs. Documentation: doc/tools/SERViewer/SERViewer.html and doc/tools/PlanetaryStack/PlanetaryStack.html.</p>
         </description>
      </package>
   </platform>
   <platform os="all" arch="noarch" version="1.8.0:2.0.0">
      <package fileName="EZStretch_v1.0.11.zip" sha1="2e9b1ae6f14fb3b0980f78caca35741facdf1779" type="script" releaseDate="20261028">
         <title>EZ Stretch</title>
         <description>
            <p>
               <b>EZ Stretch v1.0.11</b>
            </p>
            <p>Unified stretch suite combining three algorithms in a single interface.</p>
         </description>
      </package>
      <package fileName="EZDonutRepair_v1.0.4.zip" sha1="55f2bb63a65f3c57493b55f5afc4ebb27e086fc3" type="script" releaseDate="20261028">
         <title>EZ Donut Repair</title>
         <description>
            <p>
               <b>EZ Donut Repair v1.0.4</b>
            </p>
            <p>Removes donut/dust artifacts from astrophotography frames.</p>
         </description>
      </package>
      <package fileName="EZHazeKill_v1.0.2.zip" sha1="49699f7494e06db18e54d6f88245db41288f782c" type="script" releaseDate="20261028">
         <title>EZ Haze Kill</title>
         <description>
            <p>
               <b>EZ Haze Kill v1.0.2</b>
            </p>
            <p>Gradient and haze reduction.</p>
         </description>
      </package>
      <package fileName="gaia-depth-grade_v1.0.13.zip" sha1="7f736f18fa3d02511e2fd10941f567a497dfd0d2" type="script" releaseDate="20261028">
         <title>Gaia Depth Grade</title>
         <description>
            <p>
               <b>Gaia Depth Grade v1.0.13</b>
            </p>
            <p>Physically-grounded depth grade for astrophotographs using Gaia distances. Defaults to the offline local Gaia DR3 database (the one PixInsight uses for plate-solving; no network use); an online option queries the ESA Gaia archive over HTTPS for Bailer-Jones distances. Linux x64 only: needs the &quot;Gaia Depth Grade sidecar&quot; package from this repository, and StarXTerminator. Documentation, including network use, the programs it runs and the files it writes: doc/scripts/GaiaDepthGrade/GaiaDepthGrade.html.</p>
            <p>Enlarged (near) stars wear a star-shine halo whose radial shape is stacked from the frame&apos;s own bright, unsaturated, isolated stars and tinted to each star&apos;s own colour; frames with too few clean reference stars fall back to a Moffat profile, with a warning. Optional nebula depth (off by default) grades the starless layer too: dark dust and globules come forward and the diffuse glow recedes, with the overall strength calibrated by the Gaia matched-star distance spread, and Nebula depth / atmospheric / structure sliders.</p>
            <p>The sidecar program comes from PixInsight&apos;s update system (package &quot;Gaia Depth Grade sidecar&quot;); the script downloads, unpacks and installs nothing. Working files and online Gaia query results live in a private per-user directory, ~/.cache/gaia-depth-grade (mode 0700, checked before use). Earlier versions used ~/.astro-pi/gaia-depth-grade, which you may delete.</p>
         </description>
      </package>
   </platform>
</xri>
